Context: Chapter III of 'Réseaux via découpages' (1986) is an axiomatic attempt to describe a 'network' (réseau) on an underlying set L through the ways it can be cut up. The previous page lists the data. (a) A notion of 'modular' finite subsets T in Mod_f(L), generated in some sense by a family S_0 of elementary pieces; S_0 consists of 2-element subsets in the discrete case. (b) For each such T, the complement C(T) = L \ T, with an equivalence relation R_T whose classes A are the 'connected components' of the cut-up set. (c) For each class A, a frontier Ȧ ⊂ T. Grothendieck hopes the axioms will force (c) to follow from (b), and (b) to follow from the R_T for T in S_0.

This page treats the degenerate boundary case. Suppose some U is both finite-modular and cofinite-modular. Take disjoint finite modular S and T with S ∪ T finite modular, and such that every finite modular element of L lies in S or T. Then S ∪ T is a maximal finite modular part, so S ∪ T = L and L is finite. One then concludes:
- S_0 = P_2(L);
- all equivalence relations R_T are discrete;
- all frontiers Ȧ are empty.

This is recorded as 'Ex 1: discrete structure on L'. From S_0 = P_2(L) one gets:
- Mod_f(L) = P_f(L);
- C_T = L \ T for T in P_f(L);
- R_T is the discrete equivalence relation on L \ T, so the components are single points;
- Ȧ = ∅.

Finally this is promoted to an axiom: if a finite modular part T is maximal, then T = L and L is discrete. In modern terms, the axiom rules out a finite 'whole space' carrying non-trivial connectivity. It is the analogue of requiring, for a tame or graph-like space, that a finite cut-set equal to everything forces the trivial (discrete) structure. Its job is to separate the degenerate finite case from the genuinely 'continuous' networks treated next: Dec_1 (finiteness of the number of components) and Dec_2 (monotonicity of components and frontiers under refinement of cuts).
